.TH ALLOC 2
.SH NAME
allocator, heap_alloc, heap_free, heap_stats, heap_context \- the ring 3 heap
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "allocator :: proc() -> runtime.Allocator"
.B "heap_alloc :: proc(size: int, alignment := 16) -> rawptr"
.B "heap_free :: proc(p: rawptr)"
.B "heap_stats :: proc() -> (total: int, free_bytes: int, blocks: int)"
.B "heap_context :: proc() -> runtime.Context"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Allocator
answers the heap as a
.B runtime.Allocator
suitable for
.BR context.allocator .
.IR Libuser.startup ,
which every tool calls before its
.B main
runs, sets both the allocator and the temporary allocator of the context
to this: a tool has no arena, and a leak in a short program is a leak
nobody meets.
.PP
The heap is one run of memory taken from
.I segalloc
and grown by
.I segbrk
when it runs out, starting at 64 kilobytes and doubling; both are
described in
.IR segattach (2).
It is carved into blocks with a header apiece and a first-fit free list.
A freed block joins its free neighbours.
Nothing here is fast, and nothing needs to be: a tool allocates a few
thousand times and exits.
.PP
.I Heap_alloc
and
.I heap_free
are the allocator's two calls, for a caller with no context to carry an
allocator in, such as a server's contextless 9P handler or
.IR sys/libthread ,
which takes a thread's stack from here.
.I Alignment
above sixteen is refused; nothing in a tool asks for it.
.PP
The heap is shared by every process of a program built on
.IR sys/libthread ,
so it is guarded by one spinlock, held for the length of a walk down the
free list and never across a wait.
.PP
.I Heap_stats
reports the run's total size, how many bytes of it are free, and how
many blocks the list holds, for a self-test that checks the heap gave
back what it took.
.PP
.I Heap_context
builds a
.B runtime.Context
with the heap as its allocator and nothing else, for a contextless
handler that needs one: a server's 9P handler, called from the serve
loop with no context of its own.
.SH EXAMPLES
A tool takes the ordinary path, through
.IR startup :
.IP
.EX
context = libuser.startup()
lines := make([dynamic]string)
.EE
.PP
A handler with no context builds one where it needs the heap:
.IP
.EX
ctx := libuser.heap_context()
context = ctx
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/heap.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR segattach (2),
.IR fork (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Heap_alloc
answers
.B nil
when the kernel refuses to grow the run.
.I Allocator
reports
.B .Out_Of_Memory
from the same cause, through the ordinary
.B runtime.Allocator
protocol.
.SH BUGS
There is no
.B Free_All
and no
.B Query_Info
mode; both answer
.BR .Mode_Not_Implemented .
